       46  '''Briceni''' ({{IPAc-ro|lang|pron|b|r|i|'|c|e|ni}}) is a district ({{langx|ro|[[raion]]}}) in the north-west of [[Moldova]], with the administrative center at [[Briceni]]. The other major city is [[Lipcani]]. As of January 1, 2011, its population was 75,300.
       47  ==History==
       48  The first documentary attestation dates back to district towns June 17, 1429. Such localities: [[Lipcani]], [[Larga, Briceni|Larga]], [[Mihăileni, Briceni|Mihăileni]], [[Șirăuți]] are mentioned first in the period 1429–1433. In July 1429, reign of Moldova [[Alexander the Good]] Dan Uncleata had esta
       49  ==Geography==
       50  Located in the extreme northwest of the Republic of Moldova, the district borders [[Ukraine]] in the north, [[Ocnița District]] in the east, [[Edineț District]] in the south, and [[Romania]] in the west, across the river [[Prut]]. [[Criva, Briceni|Criva]] village located in the district is the weste
       51  District located on the Plateau of Moldavia, has a [[relief]] with a mean fragmentation. With a low land sliding process. Maximum altitude in the district is located near the village [[Bălcăuți, Briceni|Bălcăuți]], reaching heights of 260 m.
       52  The "Toltrele Prutului" is a chain of limestone formations in the southwestern part of the district, crossed by the rivers [[Vilia (Prut)|Vilia]] and [[Lopatnic (river)|Lopatnic]]. Although they are subject to human intervention Toltrel preserves a significant part of its former beauty, left a legac
       53  === Climate ===
       54  The climate is [[temperate]] with rainfall district 600–650 mm high, sometimes 800 mm and average temperature of {{convert|8|C|0}}. In summer the average temperature is {{convert|19.5|C|0}}, in winter {{convert|-5.5|C|0}}. After the national average district has the highest annual amount of precipit
       55  === Emil Racoviță Cave ===
       56  [[File:Zolushka cave excavations.jpg|thumb|Emil Racoviță cave excavation]]
       57  Situated in the extreme northwest of [[Moldova]], Emil Racoviță Cave has a total length of 90.2 km underground labyrinths, is the third largest [[gypsum]] cave in Europe. It was discovered in 1959 after an explosion in the village [[Criva, Briceni|Criva]] gypsum quarry. In 1977 the first scientific 
       58  === Fauna ===
       59  Fauna of the district is typical of European [[forest steppe]], characterized by: [[hare]], [[hedgehog]], [[squirrel]], [[fox]], [[stone marten]], [[weasel]], less [[deer]], [[wild boar]], [[badger]] and rarely [[elk]].
       60  Of birds, there are: [[skylark]], [[common blackbird|blackbird]], [[Old World quail|quail]], [[stork]], [[starling]], less [[partridges]] and [[pheasant]].
       61  === Flora ===
       62  Forests occupy 10.0% of district. There are: [[English oak]], [[maple]], [[birch]] and [[mugwort]]. Of plants, there are: [[fescue]], [[Urtica|nettle]], [[clover]], [[peony]] and more.
       63  === Rivers ===
       64  The main rivers that cross the district are: [[Vilia (Prut)|Vilia]], Larga, [[Lopatnic (river)|Lopatnic]] (57 km), but rivers [[Racovăț (Prut)|Racovăț]] (68 km) and [[Draghiște]] (67 km) crossing in the upper. Briceni District is in the [[Prut]] river basin. Most lakes are of artificial origin.
       65  === Protected areas ===
       66  *Complex geology and paleontology of the [[Lopatnic (river)|Lopatnic]] river basin (CGPRL)
       67  *Emil Racovita Cave
       68  *Rososeni Natural Reserve
       69  *[[Tețcani]] Landscape Reserve
